The inner limit **n - 1 - i** keeps **a[j + 1]** inside the array and skips the elements already in place.

sections.body_md:
An **array** is a composite (reference) data type: a named collection of elements of the **same data type**, stored together and reached by an **index** that starts at **0**. The **length** property gives the number of elements; the last index is length - 1.

```java
int[] a = new int[4];
double[] d = new double[3];
boolean[] f = new boolean[2];
char[] c = new char[2];
String[] s = new String[2];
System.out.println(a[0]);
System.out.println(d[1]);
System.out.println(f[0]);
System.out.println((int) c[1]);
System.out.println(s[1]);
```

Output (one value per line): `0 / 0.0 / false / 0 / null`.

- `int[] a = new int[5];` and `int a[] = new int[5];` declare and create an array; `int[] a = {72, 65, 88};` creates and fills it, and the length is 3.
- Every element starts with the default value of its type: 0 for int, 0.0 for double, false for boolean, the null character for char, null for String.
- A list of values and a size must not be given together: javac rejects `new int[3]{1, 2, 3}`.

An index outside **0 to length - 1** compiles but stops the program with an **ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException**: for an array of 5 elements, a[5] gives `java.lang.ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException: Index 5 out of bounds for length 5` and a[-1] gives `java.lang.ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException: Index -1 out of bounds for length 5`. A loop must use `i < a.length`, not <=. `a.length()` is a compile-time error: "cannot find symbol".

**Processing:** one loop gives the sum, maximum, minimum and average; start max and min with a[0].

```java
int[] visitors = {42, 35, 58, 61, 29, 45};
int sum = 0, max = visitors[0], min = visitors[0];
for (int i = 0; i < visitors.length; i++) {
    sum = sum + visitors[i];
    if (visitors[i] > max)
        max = visitors[i];
    if (visitors[i] < min)
        min = visitors[i];
}
System.out.println("Sum = " + sum);
System.out.println("Max = " + max);
System.out.println("Min = " + min);
System.out.println("Average = " + (double) sum / visitors.length);
```

Output: `Sum = 270 / Max = 61 / Min = 29 / Average = 45.0`.

**References:** an array name holds a reference. `int[] b = a;` makes two names for one array: for a = {1, 2, 3}, changing b[0] to 50 shows `50 50` for a[0] and b[0]. Copying the elements into a new array (a loop) gives `1 50`. An array passed to a method can be changed by the method.

**Linear (sequential) search** compares the key with each element in turn from the first, until a match is found or the array ends. It works on any array. The position variable starts at -1 and a **break** stops the search at the first match.

```java
int[] a = {14, 27, 9, 33, 21, 8};
int key = 33;
int pos = -1;
for (int i = 0; i < a.length; i++) {
    if (a[i] == key) {
        pos = i;
        break;
    }
}
if (pos == -1)
    System.out.println("Not found");
else
    System.out.println("Found at index " + pos);
```

Output for the key 33: `Found at index 3`; for the key 50: `Not found`.

Best case: 1 comparison (key first). Worst case: n comparisons (key last or absent): the key 8 needs 6 comparisons in this 6-element array. Without the break the loop goes on and the position ends as the **last** match.

**Binary search** works only on a **sorted** array. It compares the key with the middle element and discards the half that cannot contain it.

```java
int[] a = {5, 12, 19, 27, 34, 41, 56, 63, 78};
int key = 41;
int low = 0, high = a.length - 1, pos = -1;
while (low <= high) {
    int mid = (low + high) / 2;
    if (a[mid] == key) {
        pos = mid;
        break;
    } else if (key > a[mid])
        low = mid + 1;
    else
        high = mid - 1;
}
if (pos == -1)
    System.out.println("Not found");
else
    System.out.println("Found at index " + pos);
```

Output: `Found at index 5` (3 steps). For the absent key 30 the search ends with low greater than high after 4 steps: `Not found`.

- mid = (low + high) / 2 (integer division); key greater: low = mid + 1; key smaller: high = mid - 1; loop while low <= high.
- At most 10 comparisons for 1000 elements, against 1000 for linear search.
- An unsorted array gives wrong answers (for {40, 10, 30, 20, 50} the present key 10 shows `Not found`); a descending array needs reversed comparisons.

In **selection sort** each pass finds the index of the smallest element of the unsorted part and swaps it with the element at the start of that part. n elements need **n - 1** passes and at most one swap per pass.

```java
int[] a = {29, 14, 37, 8, 22};
int n = a.length;
for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
    int min = i;
    for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
        if (a[j] < a[min])
            min = j;
    }
    int t = a[i];
    a[i] = a[min];
    a[min] = t;
    String line = "Pass " + (i + 1) + ":";
    for (int k = 0; k < n; k++)
        line = line + " " + a[k];
    System.out.println(line);
}
```

Output (array after each pass):

```
Pass 1: 8 14 37 29 22
Pass 2: 8 14 37 29 22
Pass 3: 8 14 22 29 37
Pass 4: 8 14 22 29 37
```

For descending order, keep the index of the largest element (`a[j] > a[max]`).

In **bubble sort** each pass compares adjacent elements a[j] and a[j + 1] and swaps them if they are out of order, so the largest element moves to the end. The inner limit is **n - 1 - i**.

```java
int[] a = {29, 14, 37, 8, 22};
int n = a.length;
for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
    for (int j = 0; j < n - 1 - i; j++) {
        if (a[j] > a[j + 1]) {
            int t = a[j];
            a[j] = a[j + 1];
            a[j + 1] = t;
        }
    }
    String line = "Pass " + (i + 1) + ":";
    for (int k = 0; k < n; k++)
        line = line + " " + a[k];
    System.out.println(line);
}
```

Output (array after each pass):

```
Pass 1: 14 29 8 22 37
Pass 2: 14 8 22 29 37
Pass 3: 8 14 22 29 37
Pass 4: 8 14 22 29 37
```

For descending order use `a[j] < a[j + 1]`. A flag set when a swap happens lets the sort stop after a pass without swaps. With the inner limit n instead of n - 1 - i, the program stops with `java.lang.ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException: Index 4 out of bounds for length 4`.

**Parallel arrays:** swap both arrays at the same positions. **Selection or bubble:** both need n - 1 passes; for {29, 14, 37, 8, 22} selection sort makes 2 swaps and bubble sort 6. Sorting and searching in double-dimensional arrays is not required.

