answer.model_answer_md:
An **array** is a composite data type: a named collection of elements of the **same data type** stored one after another, each reached by an **index** that starts at 0.

```java
int[] marks = new int[5];
marks[4] = 80;
System.out.println(marks.length + " " + marks[4] + " " + marks[0]);
```

The last element of the 5 elements has the index 4. The output is `5 80 0`: the length, the stored value and the default value 0 of an element that was not assigned.

**int[] b = a;** copies only the **reference**: a and b become two names for one array, so a change made through b is seen through a.

Copying with a loop creates a **new array** (new int[a.length]) and stores each element in it, so the two arrays are independent.

With a = {1, 2, 3} and b[0] changed to 50, the first version displays `50 50` for a[0] and b[0], while the loop version displays `1 50`.

```java
int[] a = {1, 2, 3};
int[] b = new int[a.length];
for (int i = 0; i < a.length; i++)
    b[i] = a[i];
b[0] = 50;
System.out.println(a[0] + " " + b[0]);
```

```java
int[] wage = {640, 720, 580, 910, 750, 690};
int max = wage[0], at = 0;
for (int i = 1; i < wage.length; i++) {
    if (wage[i] > max) {
        max = wage[i];
        at = i;
    }
}
System.out.println("Highest wage = " + max + " at index " + at);
```

Output: `Highest wage = 910 at index 3`. The first element is taken as the starting maximum and the loop begins at index 1; the index is updated together with the maximum.

