Linear search compares the **key** with the elements one by one, starting from the first, until a match is found or the end of the array is reached. A flag or a position variable records the result.

**Advantage:** it works on an array in any order, and it is simple to write. **Disadvantage:** it is slow for a long array, because in the worst case every element is compared; for the key at the last position of a 6-element array the code below makes `6` comparisons.

```java
int[] a = {14, 27, 9, 33, 21, 8};
int key = 8, count = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < a.length; i++) {
    count++;
    if (a[i] == key)
        break;
}
System.out.println(count);
```

```java
int[] rollNo = {112, 105, 130, 118, 101, 126, 109, 121};
int key = 126;
int pos = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < rollNo.length; i++) {
    if (rollNo[i] == key) {
        pos = i + 1;
        break;
    }
}
if (pos == 0)
    System.out.println("Absent");
else
    System.out.println("Position " + pos);
```

For the key 126 the output is `Position 6`, and for the key 140 it is `Absent`. Position 0 can serve as the flag for "not found" because positions counted from 1 are never 0.

